{
  "evidence": [
    {
      "id": 1,
      "fact": "On August 6, 2014, Russia imposed a one-year ban on imports of many food and agricultural products from countries participating in sanctions against Russia, including the U.S., EU member states, Canada, Australia, and Norway.",
      "source_date": "2014-08-06"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "fact": "On June 24, 2015, President Putin signed a decree extending the counter-sanctions, keeping the food ban in place until August 6, 2016.",
      "source_date": "2015-06-24"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "fact": "On August 13, 2015, Russia expanded the embargo to include similar food products from Albania, Montenegro, Iceland, Liechtenstein, and Ukraine.",
      "source_date": "2015-08-13"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "fact": "On April 30, 2015, Deputy Prime Minister Arkady Dvorkovich stated, 'We are not currently considering canceling the ban,' while Kremlin spokesman Dmitry Peskov said the decision would depend on the EU's sanctions decision.",
      "source_date": "2015-04-30"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "fact": "On March 2, 2015, Russian authorities indicated they might possibly withdraw the ban on imports of food products from sanctioning countries only if those products were subsequently processed in Russia, a measure framed as conditional and partial rather than a full lifting of the embargo.",
      "source_date": "2015-03-02"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "fact": "In June 2015, reporting described the extension of the food import ban as retaliation for the EU's sanctions extension rather than a move toward lifting it.",
      "source_date": "2015-06-24"
    }
  ],
  "reasoning": "Based on the evidence available before the cutoff, Russia has not officially lifted any part of its food embargo on the specified countries before August 6, 2016. The embargo was extended for one year on June 24, 2015, setting a hard deadline of August 6, 2016 for the current ban [2]. High-ranking officials, including Deputy Prime Minister Arkady Dvorkovich, explicitly stated in April 2015 that they were not currently considering canceling the ban [4]. While there was a discussion in March 2015 about potentially allowing processed food products, this was a conditional, partial measure and not a lifting of the embargo on the products themselves [5]. The extension in June 2015 was characterized as retaliation for Western sanctions, reinforcing the policy's continuity rather than a move toward lifting it [6]. No official announcement or decree prior to the cutoff date indicates a full or partial lifting of the embargo for the listed countries within the specified timeframe. Therefore, the prediction is that no official lifting will occur before the deadline.",
  "probability_yes": 0.05,
  "prediction": "no"
}

