      "fact": "The Early Warning Project determined in late 2016 that no new episode of mass killing had been confirmed in Myanmar in 2016, despite the high risk assessment and documented abuses.",
      "source_date": "2016-12-30"
    }
  ],
  "reasoning": "The context indicates that while a significant escalation of violence occurred in October 2016 involving killings, rape, and arson against the Rohingya population [1][2][3], the Early Warning Project (EWP) explicitly concluded by the end of 2016 that no new episode of mass killing had been officially confirmed for that year [5]. The EWP's definition of a mass killing episode requires specific criteria that, according to their assessment, were not met by the events of late 2016, even though the abuses were severe and documented by human rights groups [2][3][4]. Since the question asks if a *new* episode will occur before January 1, 2017, and the EWP had already reviewed the 2016 data and found no such episode to have occurred by the cutoff, the prediction is that no new episode meeting the specific definition will be recorded in the remaining days of 2016.",
  "probability_yes": 0.0,
